# core.autocrlf=true rewrites line endings on checkout. For most files that is
# harmless; for these three it is not.
#
# *.csv -- family_vocab.csv is a checkpoint binding credential: the checkpoint
#   stores its sha256, and the vocab is "the only credential for embedding-row
#   identity" (CLAUDE.md Step 6). A CRLF checkout changes that sha256 without
#   changing a single family name, so the binding fails against a file whose
#   content is in fact correct. This already happened: the copy under
#   data1m/feats/100k/ was CRLF (sha 0fbf5c11...) while every T6 checkpoint
#   bound the LF form (sha f40af358...). See docs/1M/T6more.md P2-1.
#
# *.sh / *.sbatch -- these run on Linux. A CRLF shebang line is a
#   "bad interpreter: ^M" failure at job start, after the queue wait.
*.csv text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.sbatch text eol=lf
